hadiths[44]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1828","´Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that` three men came to the houses of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask about the worship of the Prophet (peace be upon him). When it was described to them, they considered his worship to be little (that is, they considered it insufficient for themselves). Then they said, ""What comparison do we have with the Prophet (peace be upon him)? All his past and future sins have been forgiven."" One of them said, ""I will pray all night."" The second said, ""I will fast continuously."" The third said, ""I will not marry (that is, I will stay away from women)."" Meanwhile, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came and said: ""Did you say such and such things? Listen! I am the one among you who fears Allah the most and is most mindful of His rights, but I fast and I break my fast, I pray (at night) and I sleep, and I also marry women. (Beware!) Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not from me."
"1829","The conqueror of Persia, Sayyiduna Sa’d bin Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), used to say: The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade ‘Uthman bin Maz’un (may Allah be pleased with him) from abstaining from marriage; if he had permitted him, we would have become eunuchs."
"1830","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): I said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), ""I am a young man and I fear that I may commit adultery, and I do not have the means to marry."" But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not reply to me. I repeated my request in the same manner, but he remained silent. I asked again, but he did not answer. When I repeated it once more, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) replied: ""Abu Huraira! The pen has dried (after writing) what is decreed for you (your destiny is already written and cannot be changed), whether you become castrated or not."
"1831","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: I asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! If you stop at a place where there are trees, some of which have already been grazed and some have not been grazed at all, from which tree would you let your camel graze?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) replied: ""From the one that has not been grazed."" Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) meant by this that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not marry any virgin woman other than her."
"1832","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) sent a marriage proposal to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) for me. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""I am your brother."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) replied, ""You are my brother in Allah's religion and His Book, and she (Aisha) is lawful for me."
"1833","Narrated from Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that Abu Hudhaifah bin Utbah bin Rabi’ah bin Abd Shams, who was present at the Battle of Badr, adopted Salim as his son and married him to his niece Hind, daughter of Walid bin Utbah Rabi’ah. Salim was a slave of an Ansari woman, just as the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) had adopted Zaid as his son. It was the custom in the time of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) that if someone adopted a person as a son, people would call him by the name of his adoptive father, and after his death, he would also inherit from him. When Allah revealed this verse: “Call them by (the names of) their (real) fathers...” (Al-Ahzab: 5), then all of them began to be called by the names of their real fathers. And if his father was not known, then he would be called a Mawla (client) or a religious brother. Afterwards, Sahla bint Suhail bin Amr al-Qurashi al-Amiri, who was the wife of Abu Hudhaifah, came to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and said, “O Messenger of Allah! We used to consider Salim as our son, but now Allah has sent down the command which you know (what should I do?)” Then the entire hadith was narrated."
"1834","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went to Dubaa’ah bint Zubair (ibn Abdul Muttalib) and asked her: “Do you intend to perform Hajj?” She said (yes), but I am suffering from severe pain. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Then go for Hajj and stipulate (the condition) that: O Allah, my place of coming out of Ihram is where You restrain me (due to any excuse such as illness, etc.).” And Dubaa’ah (may Allah be pleased with her) was married to Miqdad bin Aswad (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1835","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""A woman is married for four reasons: (1) for her wealth, (2) for her lineage, (3) for her beauty, (4) for her religiousness. So you should marry the religious one, otherwise your hands will be dust-covered."
"1836","Sahl (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that a wealthy man passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), so the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""What do you say about this man?"" They replied, ""He is such a person that if he proposes (for marriage), his proposal will be accepted; if he intercedes for someone, his intercession will be accepted; and if he speaks, people listen attentively."" (Sahl, may Allah be pleased with him, says) Then the Prophet remained silent. After that, another man who was poor and needy among the Muslims passed by, so the Prophet (peace be upon him) asked, ""What do you say about this man?"" They replied, ""He is such a person that if he proposes (for marriage), his proposal will not be accepted; if he intercedes for someone, his intercession will not be accepted; and if he speaks, people do not listen attentively."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If the earth were filled with such wealthy people, even then this poor man is better than all of them."
"1837","Sayyiduna Usama bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""I have not left behind me any trial more harmful to men than women."
"1838","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that someone said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Why do you not marry (the leader of the martyrs, Sayyiduna) Hamza's (may Allah be pleased with him) daughter? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied: ""She is my niece through breastfeeding."
"1839","Narrated from Ummul Momineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that she heard the voice of a man who was seeking permission to enter the house of Ummul Momineen Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her). Ummul Momineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, indeed this (non-mahram) man wants to enter your house."" So the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I know that this is so-and-so, who is Hafsa's (may Allah be pleased with her) foster uncle."" Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) asked: ""If so-and-so, who was my uncle through milk relationship, were alive, would I appear before him?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Yes! The relationships that are forbidden by lineage are also forbidden by suckling (milk relationship)."
"1840","Umm al-Mu’minin Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with her) says: I said, “O Messenger of Allah! Marry my sister, the daughter of Abu Sufyan.” He asked, “Do you like that?” (Do you not dislike having a co-wife?) I said, “Yes.” (But) even now, I am not your only wife, and I do not dislike my sister sharing goodness with me.” He said, “It is not lawful for me (to have two sisters in marriage at the same time).” I said, “We have heard that you want to marry the daughter of Abu Salamah?” The Prophet (peace be upon him) asked, “The daughter of Umm Salamah? (Do you mean I want to marry her?)” I said, “Yes.” So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Even if she were not my stepdaughter (the daughter of my wife from a previous husband), she would still not be lawful for me, because she is my niece through breastfeeding. Both Abu Salamah (her father) and I were breastfed by Thuwaibah (may Allah be pleased with her). (O lady!) Do not present your sisters and daughters to me (they are not lawful for me).”"
"1841","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) came to her, and at that time a man was sitting with her. Seeing this, the blessed face of the Prophet (peace be upon him) changed, as if he disliked it. I said, ""This is my foster brother."" So the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Consider carefully who your brothers are, because the relationship of fosterage is only established when the child’s nourishment (is milk)."
"1842","Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a woman be married together with her paternal aunt or her maternal aunt."
"1843","Narrated Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade Shighar marriage (which means that a man gives his daughter or sister in marriage to another man on the condition that the latter gives his daughter or sister in marriage to the former, and no dowry is fixed for either of them)."
"1844","Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah and Sayyiduna Salamah bin Akwa’ (may Allah be pleased with them) say: We were in an army (which went to Hunain), when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) came to us and said: You are permitted to do Mut’ah, so do Mut’ah. Ibn Abi Dhi’b narrates that Iyas bin Salamah bin Akwa’ narrated from his father that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “If a man and a woman do Mut’ah and do not specify a period, then (at least) they should stay together for three days and three nights. After that, if they wish, they may separate or extend the period.” Salamah said: “I do not know whether this permission was specific for us or for all people.” Imam Bukhari clarified that Sayyiduna Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said that this ruling was abrogated at the battle of Khaybar. (See Hadith: Book: On the Description of the Battles... Chapter: Description of the Battle of Khaybar...)"
"1845","Sahl bin Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that a woman presented herself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (for marriage), but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not respond. Then a man said to him, “O Messenger of Allah! Marry her to me.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked, “What do you have (to give as a dowry)?” He replied, “I have nothing.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “Go and look, even if it is an iron ring.” He went and then returned and said, “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah! I found nothing, not even an iron ring, but I have this lower garment, give her half of it.” Sahl (may Allah be pleased with him) says that he did not have another sheet. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied, “What will she do with your garment? If you wear it, she will get nothing from it, and if she wears it, you will get nothing from it.” The poor man sat down (disappointed), and after sitting for a long time, he stood up (to leave). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him (leaving) and called him back himself or had someone call him, and said, “Which surahs of the Qur’an do you know by heart?” He counted several surahs and said, “I know such and such surahs.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “We have given her to you in marriage in exchange for the Qur’an (that you know by heart),” (meaning you will teach her those surahs and that will be your dowry)."
"1846","A different narration from Sayyiduna Sahl bin Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) states that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, “O Messenger of Allah! I have come to offer myself to you.” The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) looked at her, then looked at her from head to toe, then lowered his head... Then the previous hadith was narrated in full, and at the end it was said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Can you recite (these surahs) by heart?” He said, “Yes.” The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “Go! I have made you the owner of this woman in exchange for what you know of the Qur’an.” (That is, teaching these surahs will be your dowry.)"
"1847","Narrated Sayyiduna Ma'qil bin Yasar (may Allah be pleased with him): I married my sister to a man, and he divorced her. When her 'iddah was completed, he again sent a proposal for marriage. I replied to him, ""I had married her to you and made her your wife, and honored you, but you divorced her. Now you are sending a proposal again? By Allah! She will never return to you."" That man was not bad (he was righteous), and my sister was also willing to return to him. At that time, Allah the Exalted revealed this verse: ""Do not prevent them from marrying their (former) husbands"" (Al-Baqarah: 232). I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Now (since Allah's command has come), I will surely comply (and marry her to him)."" Then he married his sister to him. (Note: In the blessed verse, the address is to the guardians (such as father, brother, etc.) of the woman, because it is their responsibility to arrange the marriage. If the guardian's permission was not necessary, the address would not have been to the guardians of the woman.)"
"1848","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A widow should not be married without her permission, nor should a virgin be married without her permission."" The people asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! How is the permission of a virgin?"" He (peace be upon him) said: ""Her silence is her consent."
"1849","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! A virgin girl feels shy."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Her silence is her consent."
"1850","Narrated from Sayyidah Khansa bint Khidam Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) that her father married her off while she had previously been married (i.e., she was not a virgin). She was displeased with this marriage, so she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned this. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) then annulled the marriage that her father had arranged."
"1851","Narrated Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a person should bid over the bid of his brother, and that a man should not send a marriage proposal over the proposal of his brother until the first suitor leaves the proposal or gives permission to the second man."
"1852","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""It is not permissible for a woman to ask her husband to divorce her sister (co-wife), so that she may pour out her share of the cup herself; this cannot be, she will get only what is destined for her."
"1853","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that she took a bride to an Ansari man, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Aisha, did you not have any entertainment with you? (Look!) The Ansar are pleased with entertainment."
"1854","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If anyone, when approaching his wife, says these words of supplication: 'In the name of Allah. O Allah! Protect me from Satan and keep Satan away from the offspring You grant us,' then if they are destined to have a child, Satan will not be able to harm him."
"1855","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) did not hold a wedding feast for any of his wives like he did for Umm al-Mu'mineen Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her). He (peace be upon him) held her wedding feast with a goat."
"1856","Lady Safiyyah bint Shaybah (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the wedding feast (walimah) for some of his wives with two mudd of barley."
"1857","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When any of you is invited to a wedding feast, he must surely go."
"1858","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should not harm his neighbor, and I advise you to treat women well, for women were created from a rib, and the most crooked part of the rib is its upper part. If you try to straighten it, you will break it, and if you leave it, it will remain crooked, so treat women well. I advise you to treat women well."
"1859","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: (In Yemen) eleven women gathered in one place and made a pact among themselves to describe the condition of their husbands, hiding nothing. The first woman said: My husband is like the meat of a lean camel placed on the top of a mountain; neither is the path easy so that one may climb to the top, nor is the meat fat enough that one would endure hardship to bring it down. The second said: If I begin to describe my husband, where would I stop? I fear I may not be able to describe everything, but if I do, I can mention all his open and hidden faults. The third said: My husband is very tall; if I speak, I get divorced, and if I remain silent, he leaves me hanging (neither married nor divorced). The fourth said: My husband is like the night of Tihamah (moderate)—neither too hot nor too cold (i.e., always the same), neither too fearful nor too sorrowful. The fifth said: If my husband comes home, he is like a leopard, and when he goes out, he is like a lion (and so noble in temperament) that he never asks about what he has left behind. The sixth said: My husband (is such a glutton) that if he eats, he finishes everything, and if he drinks, he gulps it all down, and when he sleeps, he lies alone; he never even reaches out his hand towards my stomach, nor does he ever inquire about my joys or sorrows. The seventh said: My husband is misguided, weak-chested, and oppressive; every fault is present in him. (If he speaks) he may break my head or wound me, or do both. The eighth said: My husband’s touch is like that of a rabbit (i.e., he has a delicate body), and his fragrance is like that of zar’nab (a kind of grass). The ninth said: My husband is of high status (noble), with long soles, very generous; his house is near the assembly (a man of opinion). The tenth said: My husband’s name is Malik (owner of property), and how can I praise Malik (he is above all the praises that come to mind)? At his house, there are many camels (for guests), and they rarely go to pasture, and when they hear the sound of the drum, they are sure they will be slaughtered (for guests). The eleventh said: My husband is Abu Zar’ and what can I say about him! He made my ears heavy with jewelry and filled my arms with fat, and kept me so happy that I began to praise him. He fed me well and made me fat, so much so that I considered myself very beautiful and plump. He found my family to be such that they barely had a few goats (I was poor), then brought me into such a prosperous family that had horses and camels (i.e., they had both), oxen to plow and men to thresh grain (all were there). In his house, if I spoke, no one would find fault with me, and if I slept, I would sleep till morning, and if I drank water, I would drink with great satisfaction. Abu Zar’s mother, i.e., my mother-in-law, she too was a very capable woman; her wardrobe was always full, and her house was spacious. Abu Zar’s son, how good and delicate-bodied he was, thin and slender, he could sleep in the space of a drawn sword, and his food was so little that the foreleg of a four-month-old goat would fill his stomach. Abu Zar’s daughter! Subhan Allah! Obedient to her parents, plump, so much so that she could fill her cloak. (Her appearance and character were such that) she was always a cause of anger and jealousy for her co-wife. Abu Zar’s maidservant, she too was praiseworthy; she never spread our secrets, always kept the house’s secrets hidden, never stole food, never left trash in the house, always kept it clean and tidy. One day it happened that Abu Zar went out at a time when the milk vessel was being churned. When he went out, he saw a woman with two children like leopards playing with two pomegranates under her arms. Seeing her, he divorced me and married her. After that, I married a noble man who rode a swift horse and carried a sharp spear in his hand. He gave me many blessings and gave me a pair of every kind of livestock and said, “O Umm Zar’, eat yourself and feed your relatives as well (i.e., he allowed me to be generous).” Umm Zar’ says: Even if I gather all that he gave me, it would not equal even a small vessel of Abu Zar’. Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says: (After hearing this whole story) the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “I am to you as Abu Zar’ was to Umm Zar’.” (Benefit: In one narration, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Abu Zar’ divorced Umm Zar’, but I will not do so.” Upon this, Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) replied: “May my parents be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah! You treat me with even more kindness, love, and affection than Abu Zar’ did.” (Fath al-Bari: 9/276))"
"1860","Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) that he said: ""It is not permissible for a woman to observe (voluntary) fasting when her husband is present except with his permission, nor should she allow anyone to enter his house without his consent. And if a woman spends (in the way of Allah) from her husband's wealth without his order, then he will get half of the reward."
"1861","Narrated by Sayyiduna Usama bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""I stood at the gate of Paradise and saw that most of those who entered it were the poor, while the wealthy were held back at the gate (for reckoning), except for those who were commanded to be sent to Hell. Then I stood at the gate of Hell and saw that generally, most of those who entered it were women."
"1862","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates that whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, he would draw lots among his wives. On one journey, the names of Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) and Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) came up, and it was the blessed habit of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when he traveled at night, he would walk and talk with Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her). Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), ""Tonight, you ride my camel and I will ride your camel; you look at my camel and I will look at yours."" Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) agreed. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the camel of Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), while Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) was sitting on it. He greeted Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) and then moved on. When they stopped at the destination and Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) did not find the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she put both her feet in the grass (idhkhar) and said, ""O Lord! Set a snake or a scorpion upon me so that it may bite me, and I may no longer have the strength or opportunity to complain to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1863","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: If I wished, I could say that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, but Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said that it is the Sunnah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when a man marries a virgin after a widow, he should stay with her for seven days (then alternate between them), and when he marries a widow in the presence of a virgin, he should stay with her for three days (then alternate between them)."
"1864","Narrated from Sayyida Asma (may Allah be pleased with her) that a woman asked, ""O Messenger of Allah! I have a co-wife. If I show off to her that my husband gives me more than he actually does, just to make her jealous, will I be sinful?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The one who pretends to have been given what was not given to her (as a deception) is like someone who wears two garments of falsehood."
"1865","Lady Asma bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her) narrates: When Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) married me, he had no wealth, no land, nor any slaves, except for a camel that brought water and a horse, and nothing else. I used to graze his horse, give it water, sew his bucket, and knead the dough. I did not know how to bake bread, so my Ansari neighbors would bake bread for me; they were very virtuous women. I used to carry date stones on my head from Zubair’s land, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had given him, and that place was two miles away from me. One day, as I was coming with the date stones on my head, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) met me. He was accompanied by some Companions. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) called me and made the camel kneel down to seat me behind him. I felt shy to walk with men, and I remembered Zubair’s sense of honor, for he was a very jealous man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) realized that Asma felt shy, so he moved on. I went to Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) and told him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had met me while I was carrying the load of date stones on my head, and he was accompanied by his Companions. He made the camel kneel down to seat me, but I felt shy and I know your sense of honor. He said, “By Allah! For you to be seen by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) carrying date stones on your head is more unpleasant to me than you riding with him.” Afterwards, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent me a servant, who began to look after the horse, as if he had set me free."
"1866","Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Allah has a sense of honor, and Allah's sense of honor is provoked when a believer does something which Allah has forbidden."
"1867","Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: ""I know when you are pleased with me and when you are angry with me."" Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says that I asked: ""How do you recognize this, O Messenger of Allah?"" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When you are pleased with me, (while swearing an oath) you say, 'By the Lord of Muhammad,' and when you are angry with me, you say, 'By the Lord of Ibrahim.'"" Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says that I said, ""Yes, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah! I only leave your name (but not your love)."
"1868","Narrated by Sayyiduna Uqbah bin Amir (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Avoid going to women (in seclusion)."" An Ansari man said, ""What about the brother-in-law?"" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The brother-in-law is death (i.e., one should be even more cautious of him)."
"1869","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A woman should not describe another woman to her husband in such a way as if he is seeing her openly (lest any temptation arises)."
"1870","Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you have been away from home for a long time, do not return to your house at night."
"1871","Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you return home at night, do not go to your family until the woman whose husband was absent has shaved (i.e., purified herself), and the one whose hair is disheveled has combed it."
